   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "import seaborn as sns\n",
    "\n",
    "%matplotlib inline\n",
    "\n",
    "sns.set(style=\"darkgrid\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 0. Preparation (1 point) "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Task 1 (1 point)**. We will be working with data from the competition [New York City Taxi Trip Duration](https://www.kaggle.com/c/nyc-taxi-trip-duration/overview), in which it was necessary to predict the duration of a taxi trip. Download the training sample from this competition and upload it:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {
    "ExecuteTime": {
     "end_time": "2020-09-26T17:03:41.260445Z",
     "start_time": "2020-09-26T17:03:37.878813Z"
    }
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "%%time\n",
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "\n",
    "train_data = pd.read_csv('train.csv')\n",
    "test_data = pd.read_csv('test.csv')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "test_data.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data.columns"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "test_data.columns"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Notice the ``pickup_datetime`` and ``dropoff_datetime columns``. The columm ``dropoff_datetime`` was added by the organizers only to the training set, that is, this column cannot be used, let's remove it. The ``pickup_datetime`` contains the date and time of the start of the trip. To make it convenient to work with it, let's convert dates to ``datetime`` objects. \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data.drop('dropoff_datetime',\n",
    "  axis='columns', inplace=True)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data['pickup_datetime'] =  pd.to_datetime(train_data['pickup_datetime'])\n",
    "test_data['pickup_datetime'] =  pd.to_datetime(test_data['pickup_datetime'])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "type(train_data.pickup_datetime[1])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The ``trip_duration`` column contains the target value that we want to predict. Let's take a look at the distribution of the target in the training set. To do this, draw its histogram: "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "sns.distplot(train_data['trip_duration'], kde=False)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Question**: *What can be said about the target variable from the histogram of its values*?"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data['trip_duration'].quantile([0.25,0.5,0.75,0.99, 1])"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sns.distplot(train_data.trip_duration[train_data['trip_duration'] < 3440], kde=False)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In the competition, the quality metric was RMSLE:\n",
    "$$\\text{RMSLE}(X, y, a) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\ell}\\sum_{i=1}^{\\ell} \\big(\\log{(y_i + 1)} - \\log{(a(x_i) + 1)}\\big)^2}$$\n",
    "\n",
    "**Question**: *Why do you think the authors of the competition chose RMSLE and not RMSE?*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There are several linear regression models in ``sklearn``, but each optimizes mean squared error (MSE) rather than RMSLE. Let's do the following trick: we will not predict the target variable, but its *logarithm*. Denote $\\hat{y}_i = \\log{(y_i + 1)}$ — modified target, а $\\hat{a}(x_i)$ — model forecast, trained on $\\hat{y}_i$, that is logarithm of target. To predict the original value, we can simply take the exponent from our prediction: $a(x_i) = \\exp(\\hat{a}(x_i)) - 1$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Question**: Show that RMSLE optimization for model $a$ is equivalent to MSE optimization for model  $\\hat{a}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Proof**: ╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "\n",
    "So, we were able to reduce the RMSLE optimization problem to an MSE optimization problem that we know how to solve! In addition, the target logarithm has another useful property. To see it, add the column ``log_trip_duration`` to our sample (use ``np.log1p``) and draw a histogram of the modified target for the training sample. Delete the column with the old target. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "%%time\n",
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(15,10))\n",
    "train_data['log_trip_duration'] = np.log1p(train_data['trip_duration'].values)\n",
    "plt.hist(train_data['log_trip_duration'].values, bins=100)\n",
    "plt.xlabel('log(trip_duration)')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('number of train records')\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "To have some point of reference, let's calculate the value of the metric at the best constant prediction :"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "# time distribution\n",
    "print(\"Im secunds:\", np.exp(5), np.exp(9))\n",
    "print(\"In minutes:\", np.exp(5) // 60, np.exp(9) // 60)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# longest trip in hours\n",
    "train_data['trip_duration'].max() // 3600 "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Part 1. Learn `pickup_datetime` (2 points)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Task 2 (0.25 points)**. First, let's see how many trips there were on each of the days. Build a graph of the dependence of the number of trips on the day of the year (for example, you can use  `sns.countplot`):"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from datetime import datetime"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data['dow'] = train_data.pickup_datetime.dt.dayofweek\n",
    "train_data['month'] = train_data.pickup_datetime.dt.month\n",
    "train_data['hour'] = train_data.pickup_datetime.dt.hour\n",
    "train_data['doy'] = train_data.pickup_datetime.dt.dayofyear"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
    "ax = sns.countplot(data=train_data, x='doy')\n",
    "fig.set_size_inches(18.5, 10.5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Question**: You have probably noticed that there are 2 periods on the graph with abnormally small amounts of trips. Calculate on what dates these downward jumps took place and find information about what was happening in New York these days.\n",
    "\n",
    "Draw graphs of the dependence of the number of trips on the day of the week and on the hours per day (use  `sns.relplot`):"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train_data.groupby(by='doy').agg({'id':'count'}).reset_index()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import plotly.express as px"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "px.line(grouped, x='doy', y='id')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data[(train_data.doy >22) & (train_data.doy < 25)]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data['anomaly'] = train_data.doy.between(23, 24).apply(int)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sum(train_data.anomaly)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Task 3 (0.5 points)**. Draw on one graph the dependence of the number of trips on the hour in a day for different months (different curves corresponding to different months, paint in different colors, use the `hue` in` sns.relplot`). Similarly, draw the dependence of the number of trips on the hour in a day for different days of the week. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train_data.groupby(by='dow').agg({'id':'count'}).reset_index()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sns.relplot(data=grouped, x='dow', y='id')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "px.line(grouped, x='dow', y='id')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train_data.groupby(by=['hour']).agg({'id':'count'}).reset_index()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sns.relplot(data=grouped, x='hour', y='id')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "px.line(grouped, x='hour', y='id')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train_data.groupby(by=['dow', 'hour']).agg({'id':'count'}).reset_index()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "sns.relplot(data=grouped, x='hour', y='id', hue='dow')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "px.line(grouped, x='hour', y='id', color='dow')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "data1 = pd.DataFrame({'hour': train_data.hour, 'month': train_data.month, 'trip_duration': train_data['trip_duration']}).groupby([ 'hour', 'month']).sum().reset_index()\n",
    "sns.relplot(data=data1, x='hour', y='trip_duration', hue=\"month\", kind=\"line\", height=10)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Question**: What conclusions can be drawn based on the graphs above? Are any days of the week highlighted? Months? Times of Day? What is the reason for this?\n",
    "\n",
    "**Task 4 (0.5 points)**. Divide the sample into training and test in a ratio of 7:3. Using the training sample, draw a graph of the average logarithm of travel time versus the day of the week. Then do the same, but for an hour in a day and a day in a year. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "not_use_cols = ['id', 'vendor_id', '']\n",
    "use_cols = ['passenger_count', 'pickup_longitude', 'pickup_latitude', 'dropoff_longitude', 'dropoff_latitude',\n",
    "           'store_and_fwd_flag', 'dow', 'doy', 'hour', 'month', 'anomaly', 'trip_duration', 'log_dur']"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train_data['log_dur'] = np.log(train_data.trip_duration + 1)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "train, test = train_test_split(train_data[use_cols], test_size=0.3)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train.groupby(by='dow').agg({'log_dur':'mean'}).reset_index()\n",
    "px.line(grouped, x='dow', y='log_dur')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train.groupby(by='hour').agg({'log_dur':'mean'}).reset_index()\n",
    "px.line(grouped, x='hour', y='log_dur')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "grouped = train.groupby(by='doy').agg({'log_dur':'mean'}).reset_index()\n",
    "px.line(grouped, x='doy', y='log_dur')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": []
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Question**: Are the graphs of the target's dependence on the day of the week and on the hour in a day similar to those for the number of trips? Why? What happens to the average target during those two anomalous periods that we saw above? Why it happens? Do you see any trend in the graph of `log_trip_duration` versus the day of the year? "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Add the following tags based on `pickup_datetime`:\n",
    "1. Day of the week\n",
    "2. Month\n",
    "3. Hour\n",
    "4. Is the period anomalous (two binary signs corresponding to two anomalous periods)\n",
    "5. Number of the day of the year "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "#╰( ͡° ͜ʖ ͡° )つ──☆*:・ﾟ\n",
    "\n",
    "df = pd.DataFrame()\n",
    "df['dayofweek'] = train_data['pickup_datetime'].apply(lambda x: x.dayofweek)\n",
    "df['month'] = train_data['pickup_datetime'].apply(lambda x: x.month)\n",
    "df['hour'] = train_data['pickup_datetime'].apply(lambda x: x.hour)\n",
